What is Maven?
Maven is a build automation and dependency management tool for Java projects.
It standardizes how Java projects are built, tested, and packaged (e.g., into JAR/WAR).
It uses a configuration file called pom.xml (Project Object Model).

1. Basic Maven Lifecycle
Maven defines a standard build lifecycle with phases. You’ll often see these in Jenkins pipelines.
Common phases:
validate → check if project is correct
compile → compile the source code
test → run unit tests
package → create JAR/WAR
verify → run integration tests
install → put artifact into local repository (~/.m2)
deploy → upload to remote repo (Nexus, Artifactory, etc.)

2. Maven Commands You’ll Use in Jenkins
mvn clean → deletes old builds (target/)
mvn compile → compiles Java code
mvn test → runs unit tests (JUnit, TestNG)
mvn package → builds JAR/WAR
mvn install → puts artifact into local Maven repo
mvn deploy → pushes artifact to Nexus/Artifactory
mvn dependency:tree → shows dependency conflicts (super important for debugging like your protobuf issue)

3. Maven Repositories
Local repo → ~/.m2/repository/
Central repo → Maven Central (default public repo)
Remote repo → Company-hosted artifact repo (e.g., Nexus, JFrog Artifactory)

4. Maven Plugins
Maven uses plugins to extend functionality. Common ones in CI/CD:
maven-compiler-plugin → compiles Java code
maven-surefire-plugin → runs unit tests
maven-failsafe-plugin → runs integration tests
maven-deploy-plugin → uploads artifacts to repo
maven-assembly-plugin / shade plugin → build fat/uber JARs

5. Understanding pom.xml
The heart of a Maven project. Key sections:
<groupId>, <artifactId>, <version> → defines the artifact coordinates
<dependencies> → external libraries your app needs
<build> → plugins and build settings
<repositories> → remote repos
<profiles> → custom build configs (e.g., dev/prod)

Example:
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>myapp</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
      <version>3.2.0</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>

6. Maven in Jenkins Pipelines
In freestyle jobs:
Use "Invoke top-level Maven targets" build step.
Example Goal: clean install -DskipTests.
In Pipeline (Jenkinsfile):
pipeline {
  agent any
  tools {
    maven 'Maven-3.9.6'  // defined in Jenkins global tools
    jdk 'JDK-17'
  }
  stages {
    stage('Build') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn clean package -DskipTests'
      }
    }
    stage('Test') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn test'
      }
    }
    stage('Deploy') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn deploy'
      }
    }
  }
}


7. Common DevOps Problems with Maven
Dependency conflicts → use mvn dependency:tree to debug
Snapshot vs Release versions → SNAPSHOT = dev builds, Release = stable
Caching issues in Jenkins agents → clear ~/.m2 if builds act weird
Artifact repo credentials → stored in ~/.m2/settings.xml or Jenkins credentials
Example settings.xml for Nexus:
<servers>
  <server>
    <id>nexus-repo</id>
    <username>jenkins</username>
    <password>${env.NEXUS_PASSWORD}</password>
  </server>
</servers>

8. Integration with Other Tools
SonarQube → mvn sonar:sonar for code quality
JaCoCo → code coverage reports
Docker → build image after Maven package
Helm/K8s → deploy Maven-built artifacts
